WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.439
Course structure is the path learners follow through the experience.

2
00:00:04.439 --> 00:00:13.090
Menus, sections, lessons, slides, blocks, and navigation patterns should help learners understand where they are and what comes next.

3
00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:15.949
A good structure creates a sense of progress.

4
00:00:15.949 --> 00:00:24.902
It also makes the course easier to maintain because related content is grouped logically instead of scattered across disconnected screens.

5
00:00:24.902 --> 00:00:30.770
Learning flow should move from context to explanation to example to practice or reflection.

6
00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:40.098
Not every module needs all of those pieces, but learners usually benefit from knowing why a topic matters before they are asked to remember details.

7
00:00:40.098 --> 00:00:42.656
Progressive disclosure is useful here.

8
00:00:42.656 --> 00:00:49.803
Instead of showing everything at once, reveal information in manageable layers when the learner is ready for it.

9
00:00:49.803 --> 00:00:53.113
Screen layout matters because it shapes attention.

10
00:00:53.113 --> 00:00:59.809
Visual hierarchy tells learners what to read first, what supports the main point, and what action to take.

11
00:00:59.809 --> 00:01:05.301
Use headings, spacing, contrast, grouping, and consistent patterns to guide the eye.

12
00:01:05.301 --> 00:01:09.213
A screen should not make every element compete for attention.

13
00:01:09.213 --> 00:01:12.749
When everything is emphasized, nothing is emphasized.

14
00:01:12.749 --> 00:01:14.554
Avoid overcrowded screens.

15
00:01:14.554 --> 00:01:22.228
Dense paragraphs, too many icons, excessive motion, and unnecessary decorative elements can increase cognitive load.

16
00:01:22.228 --> 00:01:27.042
A strong screen supports learning by making the main idea easier to process.

17
00:01:27.042 --> 00:01:32.910
Decoration is only useful when it clarifies meaning, sets context, or improves navigation.

18
00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:37.650
The goal is not to fill a canvas; the goal is to make the learning task easier.

19
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:44.195
One practical screen-design test is to ask what the learner should notice within the first few seconds.

20
00:01:44.195 --> 00:01:51.267
If the answer is unclear, the screen may need a stronger heading, fewer competing elements, or a simpler layout.

21
00:01:51.267 --> 00:01:56.759
The best screens feel calm because the designer has already decided what matters most.
